.TH ERSSI 1 "October 2026" "erssi Shellter Edition" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
erssi \- a modular IRC client for the terminal (erssi Shellter Edition)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B erssi
[\-\-config=FILE] [\-\-home=PATH] [\-c server] [\-p port] [\-w password]
[\-n nickname] [\-h hostname] [\-!] [\-\-setup]
.br
.B erssi
\-\-update | \-\-check\-update | \-\-print\-prefix | \-v | \-?
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B erssi
is a fullscreen Internet Relay Chat client for the terminal, a fork of
Irssi. It is extended through Perl scripts and modules, and adds, among
other things, end-to-end encryption (RPE2E), a web interface (fe-web),
side panels and image previews. It keeps its own home directory,
.BR ~/.erssi ,
so it runs side by side with irssi.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-\-config=" FILE
use
.I FILE
instead of ~/.erssi/config
.TP
.BI "\-\-home=" PATH
.I PATH
specifies the home directory of erssi; default is
.B ~/.erssi
.TP
.BI "\-c, \-\-connect=" SERVER
connect to
.I SERVER
(a server address or the name of a network)
.TP
.BI "\-w, \-\-password=" PASSWORD
use
.I PASSWORD
for authentication.
.TP
.BI "\-p, \-\-port=" PORT
automatically connect to
.I PORT
on server.
.TP
.B "\-!, \-\-noconnect"
disable autoconnecting of servers
.TP
.BI "\-n, \-\-nick=" NICKNAME
specify
.I NICKNAME
as your nick
.TP
.BI "\-h, \-\-hostname=" HOSTNAME
use
.I HOSTNAME
for your irc session
.TP
.B "\-\-setup"
ask the first-run setup questions (network, nick, SASL account and theme)
again, on an existing configuration too. The setup only adds to the
configuration: networks set to autoconnect and SASL accounts saved earlier
are kept (a new SASL account for the same network replaces its old one).
Without this option the questions are asked only on a terminal,
when the configuration file does not exist yet and none of
.BR \-\-config ,
.BR \-\-connect ,
.B \-\-noconnect
and
.B \-\-nick
is given. Each answer waits at most 5 minutes; "n", end of input or no
answer skips the setup, and the configuration is saved as it is; Ctrl-C
ends erssi without saving, so the next start asks again. Without a terminal
.B \-\-setup
prints an error and exits with status 1.
.TP
.B "\-\-update"
update erssi to the newest release that carries a valid signature by the
release key of this installed copy and is a newer version, and install it
into the same prefix: from its ready-made package where there is one for
this system, otherwise built and tested from source. It runs the installer
of this release,
.IR <prefix> /share/irssi/shellter-install.sh ;
if that is missing, update with the installer erssi was installed with.
.B /UPGRADE
then loads the new version in a running erssi.
.TP
.B "\-\-check\-update"
only report whether a newer signed release is available; nothing is
installed.
.TP
.B "\-\-print\-prefix"
print the installation prefix this erssi takes its modules, help, themes
and scripts from, and exit. Used by the installer and the tests.
.TP
.B "\-v, \-\-version"
display the version of erssi and exit
.TP
.B "\-?, \-\-help"
show a help message and exit
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.erssi/config
personal configuration file, written readable only by you
.TP
.I ~/.erssi/default.theme
default theme
.TP
.I ~/.erssi/away.log
logged messages in away status
.TP
.I ~/.erssi/logs/
logs written by the default configuration
.TP
.I ~/.erssi/help/
your own /HELP pages, read before the installed ones
.TP
.I ~/.erssi/scripts/
default scripts directory
.TP
.I ~/.erssi/scripts/autorun/
directory containing links to scripts that should be loaded
automatically on startup
.TP
.I ~/.erssi/rpe2e/keyring.json
end-to-end encryption keyring
.TP
.I ~/.erssi/startup
file containing a list of commands to execute on startup
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR irssi (1).
erssi has a solid amount of documentation available: check /HELP, or the
documentation at https://github.com/y-o-o-z/erssi
